  2. Technical Accuracy (5 marks)
  
  What to say about each technical decision:
  
  Why 3 independent agents?
  
  │ "Independence is the core guarantee. Each agent uses a different provider and different search index — DuckDuckGo for general web, Wikipedia
  for reference facts, Crossref for scholarly papers. They get the same query verbatim, no suffix modification, so any disagreement is genuine,
  not induced."
  
  Why NLI for comparison?
  
  │ "We use Natural Language Inference to measure semantic contradiction between claims — not keyword matching. The score ranges 0 to 1. We gate
  debate at threshold 0.70. Agreement requires ALL pairs above entailment threshold. Disagreement needs only ONE pair above contradiction
  threshold. This asymmetry is deliberate — you need strong evidence to claim agreement, but one contradiction is enough to trigger debate."
  
  Why 5 conflict types?
  
  │ "Each type needs a different debate strategy. A temporal conflict needs to check which date the query asked about. A contextual conflict
  should check whether both claims are correct under different scopes before declaring one wrong. Using a generic strategy for all types would
  give wrong resolutions."
  
  Why Propose → Critique → Revise?
  
  │ "This mirrors peer review. The proposer makes an initial argument. Two critics challenge it. The reviser incorporates valid critiques. Early
  stopping kicks in when rounds converge — if the reviser's output matches the previous round at 95% Jaccard similarity, debate stops. This
  prevents wasted compute."
  
  Why a gate at all?
  
  │ "Standard MAD always debates. Our system only debates when evidence disagrees. On agreement queries, we save the 6-8 LLM calls a full debate
  costs. This is the core efficiency contribution."
  
