• Complete Project Explanation — Evidence-MAD
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  What is this project trying to solve?
  
  When you ask a question like "When was India's NEP introduced?", different websites give different answers — one says 1986, another says 2020. A
  normal AI just picks one and answers. This project does something smarter:
  
  1. It sends 3 independent agents to search independently
  2. It compares what each found
  3. If they agree → answer directly
  4. If they disagree → run a structured debate to figure out who is right
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  The Big Picture Flow
  
  You type a question
          ↓
  3 agents search independently (parallel)
          ↓
  Each agent's result is cleaned into one claim
          ↓
  NLI compares all pairs of claims
          ↓
  AGREE? → Answer directly, skip debate
  DISAGREE? → Classify conflict type → Run debate → Judge → Answer
  UNCERTAIN? → Report both findings
  INSUFFICIENT? → Report failure
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  File-by-File, Function-by-Function
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  app.py — The Brain / Main Pipeline
  
  This is the file you run. python3 app.py --demo starts here.
  
  MADSystem class — the entire pipeline lives here. When you create it, it sets up every component.
  
  MADSystem.solve(query) function — this is the main function. Every time you type a question, this runs. It does these steps in order:
  
  1. Calls the router to get evidence from agents
  2. Normalizes each agent's raw results into one clean claim
  3. Calls the aggregator to compare claims and decide state
  4. If INSUFFICIENT → retries failed agents once
  5. Branches into AGREEMENT / DISAGREEMENT / UNCERTAIN / INSUFFICIENT
  6. Saves a trace file to results/
  7. Returns the trace
  
  run_interactive() function — the CLI loop. Reads your keyboard input, calls solve(), prints the 4-step display you see in the terminal.
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  config.py — All Settings
  
  Reads from your .env file. Every threshold, model name, and API key lives here as a Settings class using pydantic_settings.
  
  Key settings:
  
  - LLM_API_KEY — your Groq API key
  - LLM_MODEL — currently qwen/qwen3.8-27b on Groq
  - DISAGREEMENT_THRESHOLD = 0.70 — contradiction score must exceed this to trigger debate
  - ENTAILMENT_THRESHOLD = 0.70 — all pairs must exceed this for agreement
  - NLI_MODE = mock — uses pattern-based NLI (fast, no model download). Set to transformers for real NLI model
  - MAX_DEBATE_ROUNDS = 3 — debate runs at most 3 rounds
  
  settings.validate() function — called on startup. Raises an error immediately if LLM_API_KEY is empty in LIVE mode, rather than letting it fail
  silently later.
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  run_mode.py — FIXTURE vs LIVE
  
  RunMode enum — two values: FIXTURE and LIVE.
  
  - LIVE mode (python3 app.py) — real internet search, real LLM calls. Needs API key.
  - FIXTURE mode (python3 app.py --demo) — all evidence comes from data/fixtures.json, no internet, no API calls. Instant responses. Used for
  demos.
  
  FixtureStore class — loads data/fixtures.json. When you ask "What is the capital of Australia?" in demo mode, it looks up that exact query
  string in the file and returns the pre-written agent results. If you ask something not in the file, it raises FixtureNotFoundError immediately
  (no silent fallback — this is intentional so you know exactly what's available).
  
  data/fixtures.json — contains 10 pre-written cases covering all scenarios: 2 agreements, 4 disagreements (one per conflict type), 1 uncertain, 1
  one-agent-fails, 1 two-agents-fail, 1 all-agents-fail.
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  The 3 Agents
  
  All agents live in agents/search_agent.py. There are exactly 3 agents, each a Python class. They all inherit from BaseAgent in
  agents/base_agent.py.
  
  Agent 1 — WebAgent (agent_id: web_agent)
  
  - Provider: DuckDuckGo HTML search
  - How it works: Sends an HTTP GET to https://html.duckduckgo.com/html/?q=your+query. Parses the HTML response using regex to extract titles,
  snippets, and links. No API key needed.
  - File: agents/search_agent.py, class WebAgent, method _duckduckgo_search()
  
  Agent 2 — ReferenceAgent (agent_id: reference_agent)
  
  - Provider: Wikipedia public API
  - How it works: Calls https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php with action=query&list=search. Wikipedia returns JSON with article titles and text
  snippets. No API key needed.
  - File: agents/search_agent.py, class ReferenceAgent, method _wikipedia_search()
  
  Agent 3 — ScholarlyAgent (agent_id: scholarly_agent)
  
  - Provider: Crossref scholarly metadata API
  - How it works: Calls https://api.crossref.org/works to search academic paper titles and abstracts. Returns DOI links and journal names. No API
  key needed. Only used for research-style queries (queries containing words like "study", "meta-analysis", "clinical trial" etc.).
  - File: agents/search_agent.py, class ScholarlyAgent, method _crossref_search()
  
  What retrieve() does (common to all 3)
  
  Each agent's retrieve(query) method (in SearchAgent class, agents/search_agent.py):
  
  1. Calls its provider's search function
  2. Gets back a list of {title, snippet, link} dicts
  3. Wraps them in Source objects (from evidence/models.py)
  4. Returns an Evidence object containing all sources
  
  Important: All 3 agents get the exact same query string verbatim — no modifications. This ensures independence.
  
  How they run in parallel
  
  agents/router.py, QueryRouter.execute() function — uses Python's ThreadPoolExecutor with 3 worker threads. All 3 agents search simultaneously.
  This is why retrieval takes ~2 seconds instead of ~6.
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  Evidence Normalization — evidence/normalizer.py
  
  Each agent returns 5 raw search results. Those are noisy — irrelevant pages, partial snippets. The normalizer converts them into one clean claim
  per agent.
  
  EvidenceNormalizer.normalize() function:
  
  Stage 1 — Relevance filter (_is_relevant() function):
  
  - Extracts content words from the query (removes stopwords like "the", "is", "what")
  - Checks each search result — if title+snippet share fewer than 1 content word with the query, discard it
  - This removes completely off-topic results (e.g. a Wikipedia article about a film when you asked about a chemical)
  
  Stage 2 — LLM normalization (_llm_normalize() function):
  
  - Sends the surviving snippets to the LLM with a prompt: "Given these snippets, write one sentence that answers this query. Also extract any
  date, version number, and scope."
  - LLM role used here: "normalize" — tracked in llm_client.call_report()
  - Returns a NormalizedClaim object containing: text (the one-sentence answer), source (which URL it came from), date, version, scope
  - If LLM says no snippet answers the query → NO_RELEVANT_RESULTS status
  
  So after normalization, instead of 5 noisy snippets per agent, you have 1 clean claim per agent with metadata.
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  Evidence Models — evidence/models.py
  
  This file defines all the data structures. Key ones:
  
  - Source — a URL with title and snippet. Everything is traceable to a source.
  - NormalizedClaim — one sentence answer + source + date + version + scope
  - AgentEvidence — one agent's full result: status (OK / NO_RELEVANT_RESULTS / RETRIEVAL_FAILED), list of claims, list of sources
  - AgentEvidence.is_usable — property that returns True only if status is OK AND claims list is not empty. The aggregator uses this to decide
  whether an agent's result counts.
  - EvidenceDecision — the output of the aggregator: state (AGREEMENT/DISAGREEMENT/UNCERTAIN/INSUFFICIENT), pairwise NLI scores, which agents are
  usable, which failed, and the top conflicting pair
  - ExecutionTrace — everything that happened in one solve: query, all agent evidence, decision, conflict, debate rounds, judge result, final
  answer, metrics, timestamps. Saved as JSON to results/
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  NLI — The Core Comparison Engine
  
  NLI = Natural Language Inference. It answers: "Does sentence A contradict sentence B?"
  
  It gives 3 scores for any pair of sentences:
  
  - contradiction_score — how much A and B conflict (0.0 to 1.0)
  - entailment_score — how much A supports B (0.0 to 1.0)
  - neutral_score — how unrelated they are (0.0 to 1.0)
  
  File: evidence/disagreement_detector.py
  
  Two implementations:
  
  MockNLIDetector (used when NLI_MODE=mock in .env):
  
  - Uses _pattern_predict() function — a set of rules
  - Detects different years (e.g. 2020 vs 1986 → contradiction 0.91)
  - Detects different version numbers (e.g. 3.13 vs 3.14 → contradiction 0.89)
  - Detects scope pairs (adults vs children → contradiction 0.91)
  - Detects negation pairs (safe vs dangerous → contradiction 0.91)
  - 3+ shared meaningful words → entailment 0.88
  - Otherwise → neutral 0.60
  - Fast, no model download, works offline
  
  NLIDetector (used when NLI_MODE=transformers):
  
  - Downloads and runs a real RoBERTa model: ynie/roberta-large-snli_mnli_fever_anli_R1_R2_R3-nli
  - Uses HuggingFace pipeline() with {"text": premise, "text_pair": hypothesis} input format
  - Runs in both directions (A→B and B→A) and takes the stronger contradiction score
  - Accurate for any sentence pair, not just known patterns
  - Slow on first run (model download ~1.5GB), fast after
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  Evidence Aggregator — evidence/aggregator.py
  
  EvidenceAggregator.decide() function — takes all agent evidence, returns one EvidenceDecision.
  
  The decision rule (written once, never duplicated):
  
  usable = agents where is_usable == True
  if len(usable) <= 1  →  INSUFFICIENT
  
  for every pair (A,B) in usable:
      run NLI → get contradiction_score, entailment_score
  
  if ANY pair contradiction_score >= 0.70  →  DISAGREEMENT
  elif ALL pairs entailment_score >= 0.70  →  AGREEMENT
  else                                     →  UNCERTAIN
  
  Why this asymmetry? Disagreement needs only 1 pair. If web says 2020 and Wikipedia says 1986, that's enough — even if scholarly agrees with web.
  Agreement needs ALL pairs — 2 agents entailing + 1 neutral is NOT agreement, it's UNCERTAIN. This makes the system conservative: it won't claim
  agreement unless all sources are consistent.
  
  Also records top_conflict — the pair with the highest contradiction score. This is what gets passed to the debate.
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  Conflict Classifier — conflict/classifier.py
  
  Only runs when state is DISAGREEMENT.
  
  ConflictClassifier.classify() function:
  
  - Sends the two conflicting claims + query to the LLM
  - Prompt asks: "Is this conflict TEMPORAL, VERSION, CONTEXTUAL, SOURCE, or FACTUAL?"
  - LLM role: "classifier"
  - Returns ClassificationResult with type, confidence, explanation, and status
  
  5 Conflict Types:
  
  - TEMPORAL — claims differ because they refer to different dates/times. Example: NEP 1986 vs 2020
  - VERSION — claims differ because they're about different software/product versions. Example: Python 3.13 vs 3.14
  - CONTEXTUAL — claims differ because they apply to different populations or conditions. Example: aspirin safe for adults, dangerous for children
  - SOURCE — claims differ because one source is authoritative (peer-reviewed) and another is not (blog). Example: MMR vaccine controversy
  - FACTUAL — direct incompatible facts about the same thing. Example: "Napoleon was 5'2" vs "Napoleon was 5'7"
  
  Fallback: If LLM fails, _classify_by_patterns() runs instead (pattern matching on years, version numbers, scope words). Returns status=FALLBACK
   so you know it wasn't an LLM result.
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  Debate — debate/ folder
  
  The debate has 4 files. It only runs on DISAGREEMENT state.
  
  How the debate works technically
  
  debate/manager.py — DebateManager.run_debate() function — orchestrates the loop:
  
  For round 1, 2, 3:
      1. Proposer generates an argument
      2. Validate it (>50 chars, not empty)  ← if invalid, stop immediately
      3. Critic generates 2 critiques
      4. Check for "no substantive objection" → stop early if critic is satisfied
      5. Reviser incorporates critiques into a revised argument
      6. Check convergence: if this revision is 95% similar to previous → stop early
  Returns DebateOutcome(status, stop_reason, rounds_used, failed_stages)
  
  Early stopping (stop_reason):
  
  - converged — revision text is 95%+ similar to last round (Jaccard similarity). No point continuing.
  - no_objection — critic explicitly says SUBSTANTIVE_OBJECTION: false. Debate is settled.
  - max_rounds — hit the 3-round cap.
  - stage_failure — LLM failed at proposer/critic/reviser → stops immediately, records which stage failed.
  
  debate/proposer.py — Proposer.propose() function
  
  Generates the initial argument. Uses CONFLICT_STRATEGIES dict — a different system prompt for each of the 5 conflict types.
  
  - TEMPORAL strategy tells the LLM: "Check the date each claim was recorded. If a claim is dated after the query's requested period, exclude it."
  - VERSION strategy: "Check if both claims refer to the same version. If different versions, both may be correct."
  - CONTEXTUAL strategy: "Check scope fields. If scopes differ, the contradiction may dissolve."
  - SOURCE strategy: "Evaluate source authority — prefer peer-reviewed over blogs."
  - FACTUAL strategy: "Compare claims directly against evidence."
  
  LLM role: "proposer"
  
  debate/critic.py — Critic.critique() function
  
  Receives the proposal and generates 2 separate critiques challenging it. Also outputs SUBSTANTIVE_OBJECTION: true/false — if false, the debate
  manager stops early. LLM role: "critic"
  
  debate/reviser.py — Reviser.revise() function
  
  Receives original proposal + 2 critiques. Produces a revised, strengthened argument that addresses the critiques. LLM role: "reviser"
  
  debate/judge.py — Judge.evaluate() function
  
  Only called if debate_outcome.status == "completed" — if debate failed partway, judge is skipped and judge_result = None.
  
  Receives: the two conflicting NormalizedClaim objects with full source metadata, the debate transcript, and the conflict type. Asks LLM: "Which
  claim has stronger evidence? Give scores." Returns JudgeResult with winner, reason, evidence_score, confidence. LLM role: "judge"
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  Output Generator — output/generator.py
  
  AnswerGenerator.generate() function — produces the final displayed answer. Four completely different shapes based on state:
  
  - AGREEMENT → LLM writes a direct answer from the agreed claims. No bullet dump. LLM role: "answer"
  - DISAGREEMENT + completed debate → "A TEMPORAL conflict was detected. Resolution: [winning claim]. Judge's reasoning: [reason]"
  - DISAGREEMENT + failed debate → "A conflict was detected but debate did not complete. Please consult sources directly."
  - UNCERTAIN → "Evidence neither clearly agreed nor conflicted. Different sources found: [list]"
  - INSUFFICIENT → "Insufficient evidence. Failed agents: [list with reasons]"
  
  Also reports independent_agents_agreeing separately from source_count — 3 agents agreeing is different from 9 URLs (which could all be the same
  3 agents × 3 sources each).
  
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  
  LLM Client — llm_client.py
  
  LLMClient class — the single shared LLM client. Currently points to Groq (https://api.groq.com/openai/v1), model qwen/qwen3.8-27b.
  
  chat(prompt, role="...") function — sends one message to the LLM, returns text response. The role parameter is required — it labels every call
  so you know exactly which component made it.
  
  chat_json(prompt, role="...") function — same but expects JSON back. Extracts JSON from the response even if the LLM wraps it in markdown code
  blocks.
  
  call_report() function — returns a dict showing how many OK and failed calls each role made. Example: {"normalize": {"ok": 3, "failed": 0},
  "classifier": {"ok": 1, "failed": 0}, "proposer": {"ok": 3, "failed": 0}, ...}
  
  LLMError exception — raised on any LLM failure (bad key, network error, bad JSON). Never silently returns a fake response. Every component that
  calls LLM lets this propagate up to the debate manager, which records the failure properly.
  
  Where LLM is used and with which role:
  
  ┌────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Role       │ Where                            │ Purpose                              │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ normalize  │ evidence/normalizer.py           │ Turn snippets into one claim         │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ classifier │ conflict/classifier.py           │ Identify conflict type               │
